      "why": "iterative is the lowest-area family (single shared datapath, no pipeline registers). It is the only family that can plausibly meet the 1 MSPS throughput constraint at minimum area, since it has the fewest flip-flops and the shortest combinational path per cycle. The error constraint (<= 2^-10) requires n_iter >= ~10 and data_width >= ~12; I narrow the ranges to the feasible band where accuracy_bits >= 10 is achievable while keeping area minimal. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0..2) since extra guard bits add area without helping meet the loose 2^-10 error floor. Rounding is included to capture the small accuracy/area trade-off."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >= 1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area as the sole selection objective. Among my assigned families (iterative, unrolled_k), iterative is strictly superior for this spec: it has the fewest flip-flops (no pipeline registers, no k-way parallel datapath) and the shortest critical path per cycle, giving the highest Fmax and thus the best chance of meeting 1 MSPS at the lowest area. unrolled_k with k>1 adds parallel adders and more registers, increasing area without helping throughput (it already produces one result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, but the area penalty is significant). Since the error constraint is relatively loose (2^-10), I focus the search on the minimal-accuracy configurations: data_width 12-16, n_iter 10-14, small guard bits. This concentrates the 100-eval budget on the narrow feasible region where the Pareto front over (area, accuracy_bits) will live. I allocate 100% of the budget to iterative because unrolled_k cannot beat it on area for this throughput-constrained spec."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established with 8 feasible designs covering the critical trade-off space. The minimum area design (6860 um^2) meets all constraints (throughput 9.43 MSPS >= 1, max_abs_err 0.000917 <= 0.000976562). The front spans accuracy_bits from 10.1 to 12.3 and area from 6860 to 9348 um^2, providing a clear trade-off curve. Since this is the final round, and the selection rule is 'min area_um2', the design at 6860 um^2 is the clear winner. No further exploration is possible or needed."
